1. d4
{ Starting move of the Grünfeld Defense setup. White aims to build a strong center. }
1... Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 d5
{ The Grünfeld move - Black challenges the center immediately. } 4. Nf3 Bg7 5.
e3
{ The key move in this simplified system. White aims for Be2, short castle, and will try to treat the position like a reverse Tarash defense. The idea is to create an IQP on d4 in exchange for active piece play. }
5... O-O 6. Be2 c6
{ The Schlechter defense - a combination of the Grünfeld and Slav. Not quite in the Grünfeld spirit because there is little point in the bishop being developed on g7 when d4 is well protected. After c6 white castles and generally gets good play with a queenside advance like b4, a4, b5. }
( 6... dxc4
{ Alternative line where black immediately creates the IQP. This leads to the main lines analyzed in the video. }
7. Bxc4 { Natural recapture. } 7... c5
{ A slight modification. This is related to the famous 5th game of the World Championship match between Petrosian and Spassky played in 1963. }
8. d5
{ If you're a bit more ambitious, you can go d5 as Petrosian did. White gets space advantage thanks to this pawn on d5. }
8... Bg4
{ Alternative to e6. After short castle then h3, black exchanges on f3, establishes blockade on d6, but white has space advantage thanks to the pawn on d5. }
( 8... e6 { After e6, black tries to undermine the pawn on d5. } 9. dxe6 Qxd1+
10. Kxd1 Bxe6 11. Bxe6 fxe6 12. Ke2
{ White has the slightly better endgame that Petrosian magnificently converted. Worth noting in this game was the maneuver Ng5-Ne4 establishing a knight on the untouchable e4 square. A very good game for chess understanding - Petrosian vs Spassky, 5th game of their 1963 match. }
) 9. O-O ) ( 6... c5
{ The Grünfeld player's typical move - attacking the center. White doesn't react and just castles, leading to the reverse Tarash where White has an extra tempo. }
7. O-O
